‎## Observation
‎When legitimate access to a platform is difficult, people search for a workaround 
‎rather than understanding the infrastructure creating the restriction.
‎
‎**Common Kenyan Workarounds:**
‎"Get someone abroad to verify you."
‎"Use a proxy."
‎"Create an account through someone in the US/UK."
‎
‎Presented as cleverness. But the underlying problem remains unresolved.
‎
‎## The Pattern
‎**Barrier → Frustration → Shortcut → Temporary Access → Dependency → Failure Risk → Rebuilding**
‎
‎The shortcut solves entry, not infrastructure.
‎It may provide access today while increasing vulnerability tomorrow.
‎
‎## Pattern Name: FOLK INFRASTRUCTURE
‎When formal infrastructure excludes people, communities develop informal methods of navigating around it.
‎
‎Some methods appear remarkably effective.
‎But effectiveness ≠ resilience.
‎
‎A system built on borrowed identity, unstable access, or policy circumvention 
‎can disappear as soon as the platform changes rules.
‎
